    90	## 4. Error control, multiplicity, and simulation
    91	
    92	### 4.1 Future-study error control (what R4 must represent)
    93	
    94	1. Two positive branches: **L** (LANGUAGE EARNS CONTINUATION) and **W**
    95	   (WORKFLOW VALUE, LANGUAGE NOT JUSTIFIED). Each receives alpha = 0.025.
    96	2. Within a branch, every statistical component (G1-G3) must pass with a valid
    97	   one-sided 97.5% bound. The branch is an intersection-union test, so no
    98	   further within-branch multiplicity correction is applied.
    99	3. The union bound limits any false positive branch claim to 0.05 overall.
   100	   This is not simultaneous coverage of every quantity.
   101	4. Precedence of published study outcomes: INVALID, then L, then W, then
   102	   TARGET NOT MET. Precedence is not error control.
   103	5. Descriptive 95% intervals may be published but cannot adjudicate a gate.
   104	6. Secondary metrics (review minutes, iterations, tokens, prevented
   105	   violations) cannot create a positive route.
   106	
   107	### 4.2 Role of simulation under v1
   108	
   109	Under v1, simulation results are **descriptive**. They cannot produce
   110	`SIZED_FEASIBLE` or `SIZED_INFEASIBLE` for any component (section 7), for two
   111	reasons: FEASIBLE AS PROPOSED and REQUIRES SEPARATE APPROVAL are unreachable in
   112	the public-proxy domain (section 7.3), and NOT FEASIBLE under v1 uses only the
   113	method-independent analytic bound in section 7.4, which needs no simulation,
   114	nuisance ranges, or estimator validation. R4 may still build and publish the
   115	simulation required by #1376; when it does, it must meet the requirements below
   116	so a later rules version can rely on it.
   117	
   118	| Requirement | Frozen content |
   119	|---|---|
   120	| Arm-level parameterization | Every scenario is specified by arm-level quantities (per-arm acceptance, correct-acceptance, and serious-escape probabilities; per-arm cost distributions), from which all contrasts are derived. Contrasts are never specified independently. Infeasible combinations (for example escape > accepted - correct) are rejected, not repaired. |
   121	| Design structure | Paired by source cluster; repository-cluster effect; reviewer effect; repeated slots within cluster; accepted and all-assigned denominators; replacement executions charged to the original slot (at most two per paired slot, infrastructure-only, symmetric across arms) |
   122	| Validity over dependence | Component bounds must be valid under arbitrary within-cluster dependence, because the cluster is the independent unit |
   123	| Undefined results | Zero correct acceptances gives infinite cost, and the cost component fails in that draw. Zero acceptances makes accepted-denominator safety undefined, and that comparison fails. Undefined draws are failures, never dropped. |
   124	| Sparse events | No method that yields a zero-width safety interval when no events occur |
   125	| Component validity | For every component, one-sided coverage at every registered boundary configuration: the Monte Carlo upper 99.5% bound on the rejection rate must be <= 0.025 (not "0.025 plus Monte Carlo error") |
   126	| Branch and family validity | Same rule for each branch (<= 0.025) and for any false positive branch (<= 0.05) at the global null, every mixed null, and branch nulls in which every other component passes with probability >= 0.99 |
   127	| Search and Monte Carlo error budget | R4 registers, before running, the finite list of K evaluations (scenarios x sample sizes x nuisance points) and uses Bonferroni-adjusted Monte Carlo bounds with total Monte Carlo error 0.01 across the list. No rerun with a new seed to resolve an indeterminate result. |
   128	| Power | Reported per branch as the probability of the correct branch outcome under the complete ordered procedure, never as a product or minimum of component powers |
   129	| Reproducibility | Pinned code commit, runtime version, lockfile hash, seeds, input and output hashes; a second run reproduces outputs within a declared tolerance |
   130	
   131	### 4.3 Registered design alternatives
   132	
   133	These are **design alternatives**, not predictions. No evidence supports any
   134	of them ([mechanism-and-resources.md](../mechanism-and-resources.md)). Each is
   135	stated at arm level. `a` is the per-slot acceptance probability, `k` the
   136	correct-acceptance probability, `e` the all-assigned serious-escape
   137	probability, and `m` the mean cost per assigned slot (with `m_A = 1`).
   138	
   139	| Scenario | Arm A | Arm B | Arm C | Derived contrasts |
   140	|---|---|---|---|---|
   141	| L global null (all L components at boundary) | k=0.85, e=x | k=0.85, e=x, m=1 | k=0.80, e=x+0.01, cost per correct = 0.50 of A and of B | C/A = C/B = 0.50; C-A = C-B = -5 pp; C level 0.80; safety +1 pp |
   142	| W global null | k=0.85, e=x | k=0.80, e=x+0.01, cost per correct = 0.50 of A | equal to B | B/A = 0.50; B-A = -5 pp; B level 0.80; safety +1 pp; C/B = 1 (L false) |
   143	| L design, least favorable | k=0.90, e=y | k=0.90, e=y | k=0.90, e=y, cost per correct = 0.40 of A and of B | C/A = C/B = 0.40; differences 0; safety 0 |
   144	| L design, most favorable | k=0.90, e=y+0.01 | k=0.90, e=y+0.01 | k=0.95, e=y, cost per correct = 0.35 of A and of B | C/A = C/B = 0.35; +5 pp; safety -1 pp |
   145	| W design, least favorable | k=0.90, e=y | k=0.90, e=y, cost per correct = 0.40 of A | equal to B | B/A = 0.40; C/B = 1 |
   146	| W design, most favorable | k=0.90, e=y+0.01 | k=0.95, e=y, cost per correct = 0.35 of A | equal to B | B/A = 0.35; +5 pp; safety -1 pp; C/B = 1 |
   147	
   148	`x` and `y` are nuisance base rates, constrained by `e <= a - k` per arm.
   149	Acceptance is registered as `a = 1` for every arm in these scenarios, so the
   150	all-assigned and accepted safety denominators coincide. A separate
   151	accepted-denominator boundary scenario uses `a = 0.95` in every arm with
   152	`e_subject = e_comparator + 0.0095`, which puts the accepted-denominator
   153	difference at exactly +1 pp. Cost distributions, dependence structures, and
   154	the values of `x` and `y` are registered by R4 before running; because
   155	simulation is descriptive under v1 (section 4.2), these choices cannot affect a
   156	classification.
   157	
   158	## 5. Positive-branch logic for the M0 classification
   159	
   160	A three-arm study must be able to resolve **both** branches. A design powered
   161	only for branch L is insufficient (Draft v3 section 6.3). Therefore:
   162	
   163	- **NOT FEASIBLE** follows when a necessary condition of **either** branch is
   164	  shown unattainable by the method-independent bound in section 7.4.
   165	- **FEASIBLE AS PROPOSED** would require branch-level power >= 0.80 for both
   166	  branches under the least-favorable registered alternatives. Under v1 it is
   167	  unreachable in the public-proxy domain (section 7.3).
   168	
   169	## 6. Source selection, eligibility, supply bounds, and missingness
   170	
   171	### 6.1 Source selection (frozen by R1, per amendment 001)
   172	
   173	**Candidate frame.** A repository is in the frame if, at the R2B′ extraction
   174	time `T`, it:
   175	
   176	1. is a public GitHub repository that is not a fork, archived, a mirror, or a
   177	   template;
   178	2. has GitHub-reported primary language `C#`;
   179	3. carries a license whose SPDX identifier is in the set R2A′ (#1373) permits;
   180	4. has at least one pull request merged in the window `[T - 365 days, T)`;
   181	   `T` is no later than 2026-10-29;
   182	5. is not on the prior-familiarity list (section 6.3).
   183	
   184	**Enumeration.** R2B′ enumerates the frame with the GitHub search API, using
   185	date-sliced queries so that no query exceeds the API's result cap. The
   186	extraction specification (queries, slices, timestamps) is committed and hashed
   187	before it runs. If the enumeration cannot be completed or its completeness
   188	cannot be checked against the API's reported totals, the frame size is
   189	uncertified and `H_enum_max` is unbounded (section 6.4).
   190	
   191	**Inventory draw.** R2B′ orders the enumerated frame by
   192	`SHA-256(salt || "/" || owner/name)` with `salt` = the lowercase hex merge
   193	commit SHA of the PR that freezes these rules, and inspects the first 30
   194	repositories in that order (all of them if fewer). Stars, activity, size, and
   195	familiarity with the code play no role. The draw estimates eligibility rates
   196	descriptively. It does **not** define supply: the supply bound in section 6.4
   197	covers the whole frame, with uninspected items counted as unresolved.
   198	
   199	### 6.2 Eligibility rules R2B′ applies
   200	
   201	A source cluster is confirmed eligible only if every criterion is confirmed:
   202	
   203	| ID | Criterion | Mechanical test |
   204	|---|---|---|
   205	| E1 | Merged change in the window | A pull request with `merged_at` in `[T - 365 days, T)`; linked issues (closing keywords) and reverts/backports that cite it join its cluster |
   206	| E2 | Production C# change | Changes at least one `.cs` file outside test paths, with a non-whitespace diff; test paths match the case-insensitive regex `(^|/)(test|tests|[^/]*\.tests?)(/|$)` |
   207	| E3 | Oracle ascertainable | **Confirmed true** if the change modifies at least one `.cs` file in a test path. E3 is **never confirmed false** under v1: absence of changed or existing test code does not show that no oracle could be authored (requirements, documentation, or an independently written oracle may exist), so every other case is **unresolved** and stays in `H_high`. This test does not require the historical change to have modified tests; an independently authored oracle may still be needed, which is R3's and the future study's concern |
   208	| E4 | Human-authored | Author account type is `User` (not `Bot`); evaluated from account type, never from names |
   209	| E5 | Not generated or formatting-only | At least one changed non-test `.cs` file is not under `obj/`, `bin/`, or a path containing `Generated`, and has a change outside whitespace |
   210	| E6 | Inside the R3 domain cells | The R3 (#1374) domain-cell rule, which R3 must freeze before R2B′ inspects data |
   211	
   212	R3 may adopt a stricter oracle rule only as a disclosed domain restriction
   213	under E6, never by redefining E3. A criterion that is confirmed false is a
   214	rule-based exclusion with a recorded reason. A criterion that cannot be evaluated (missing data, API error,
   215	ambiguous linkage, undecidable domain cell) leaves the cluster **unresolved**;
   216	it is never counted as ineligible or as eligible.
   217	
   218	### 6.3 Prior familiarity
   219	
   220	The prior-familiarity list is fixed at the R1 merge commit as: the
   221	`bench/corpus/` submodules (MediatR, Serilog, FluentValidation); every GitHub
   222	repository whose URL appears in this repository's `bench/`, `benchmarks/`,
   223	`tests/`, `docs/`, or `.gitmodules` at that commit; and every repository named in the
   224	pre-extraction familiarity declarations below.
   225	
   226	**Declarations.** Before R2B′ runs its extraction, the R2B′ record must contain
   227	two dated declarations listing every public repository known to have been
   228	studied for Calor development, including exposure that left no committed URL
   229	(for example, a repository read during an agent or chat session): one by the
   230	maintainer and one by the proposer agent of record for R2B′. The proposer's
   231	declaration is limited by the agent's lack of cross-session memory; it must
   232	state which sources were searched (this repository's history, the session
   233	logs available to it, and the maintainer's answers) and that it may be
   234	incomplete. The declaration cutoff is the extraction start time. A repository
   235	first recognized as familiar after the cutoff is moved to the familiar stratum
   236	in a disclosed correction; its counts remain in `H_high`. For the record, the
   237	R1 proposer session that drafted these rules studied no public repository
   238	other than this one. R2B′ publishes
   239	the list. Listed repositories are excluded from the frame, the inventory draw,
   240	and any future task pool. Their item counts are reported as a separate
   241	stratum and **are added to `H_high`** (section 6.4), because removing them
   242	must not manufacture scarcity. The proposer (Claude) and the reviewer (Codex)
   243	have unmeasurable pretraining exposure to popular public .NET repositories;
   244	this cannot be excluded and is disclosed in every R5 output.
   245	
   246	### 6.4 Supply bounds (from R2B′, over the whole frozen frame)
   247	
   248	R2B′ reports, for the whole frozen frame:
   249	
   250	| Quantity | Definition |
   251	|---|---|
   252	| `H_conf` | Historical clusters confirmed eligible under the frozen R3 rules, with complete provenance |
   253	| `H_unres` | Enumerated items whose eligibility is missing or indeterminate (including extraction failures for enumerated items) |
   254	| `H_enum_max` | A certified upper bound on the number of items in the frame that could form clusters, taken from a complete enumeration (for example a platform-reported total count for the window) that the frozen extraction specification names in advance. If no certified enumeration exists for any repository in the frame, `H_enum_max` is **unbounded**. |
   255	| `H_fam` | Merged pull requests in the window in prior-familiarity repositories (section 6.3); unbounded if not certified |
   256	| `H_high` | `min(H_conf + H_unres + (H_enum_max - enumerated items), H_enum_max) + H_fam`, that is, every item that is not confirmed ineligible for a recorded, rule-based reason is counted as potentially eligible; unbounded if `H_enum_max` or `H_fam` is unbounded |
   257	| `F_high` | `floor(H_high / 0.75)`: the largest final pool permitted when the pilot reservation is zero and authored clusters fill the maximum 25% weight. Authored supply is neither invented beyond that cap nor set to zero. |
   258	
   259	Clusters excluded for a recorded rule-based cause are excluded from all bounds.
   260	Missing eligibility is never counted as ineligible (that would fabricate
   261	scarcity). Historical and forward supply are reported separately; forward
   262	supply cannot substitute for G0.
   263	
   264	### 6.5 Missingness, invalid runs, and invalid epochs
   265	
   266	| Situation | Frozen treatment |
   267	|---|---|
   268	| Gate NOT MEASURABLE IN DOMAIN | Recorded as missing for that gate; never treated as passed, failed, zero, or "not applicable" |
   269	| Unbounded or uncertified `H_enum_max` | The supply route to NOT FEASIBLE is disabled |
   270	| R2B extraction run or R4 run with a pin/hash mismatch, a frozen-rule violation, an input registered after outputs were seen, or an unrecorded configuration change | **Invalid run.** Retained in the manifest with reason; not used. One rerun from the identical pre-declared specification is permitted. The first valid run binds; choosing among valid runs is prohibited. |
   271	| Upstream gate EXPIRED, REVOKED, or INVALIDATED (#1370 lifecycle rules) | Every dependent output is invalid from that time; the classification is UNADJUDICATED |
   272	| Future study (modeled by R4): failed, crashed, refused, exhausted, or unreviewed slot | Failed slot carrying its full cost; never removed from any denominator |
   273	| Future study: held-out correctness or severity evaluation missing for an **accepted** artifact | Acceptance and cost records are kept. For the bound on each component, the missing outcome is imputed adversarially to the claim: in the subject arm, not correct and serious escape; in the comparator arm, correct and no escape. If such imputations exceed 5% of accepted slots in any arm, the epoch is INVALID. |
   274	| Future study: protocol or trust defect | Epoch INVALID; affected evidence identified; no favorable old runs retained (Draft v3 section 6.4) |
   275	
   276	## 7. Classification rules
   277	
   278	### 7.1 Component states
   279	
   280	R4 reports exactly one state per **(branch, component)** pair:
   281	
   282	| State | Applies to | Meaning |
   283	|---|---|---|
   284	| `SIZED_FEASIBLE` | G1-G3 | Not available under v1 (section 4.2) |
   285	| `SIZED_INFEASIBLE` | G3.1 (both branches) | Section 7.4: the admissibility certificate is recorded and `F_high < N_MIN` |
   286	| `NOT_MEASURABLE_IN_DOMAIN` | any | Section 3 domain status |
   287	| `PROXY_ONLY` | G2, G3 | Measurable only by proxy, and not `SIZED_INFEASIBLE` |
   288	| `ROUTE_BLOCKED` | G4, G6.1 | Section 7.6 assignment step 1 |
   289	| `ROUTE_UNRESOLVED` | G4, G6.1 | Section 7.6 assignment steps 2 and 5 |
   290	| `ROUTE_MISSING` | G4, G6.1 | Section 7.6 assignment step 3 |
   291	| `ROUTE_ESTABLISHED` | G4, G6.1 | Section 7.6 assignment step 4 |
   292	
   293	### 7.2 Ordered classification procedure
   294	
   295	Apply in order; the first matching rule decides.
   296	
   297	1. **UNADJUDICATED** (process status, not a classification) if: R1 is not
   298	   `MET`; any upstream gate (R0, R2A′, R3, R2B′, R4) is not `MET` or has been
   299	   `INVALIDATED`; or the R5 countersignature (section 7.5) is absent. R5
   300	   publishes the dated reason. R5 may state which rule the evidence *would*

