   101	   or `minor`, and ends with either `VERDICT: no blocking objections` or
   102	   `VERDICT: blocking objections remain`.
   103	4. Claude dispositions every objection: **accepted and fixed** (with the
   104	   change) or **rejected** (with an explicit reason).
   105	5. Stop when a round has zero blocking objections, or after round 5.
   106	6. Each Codex call is appended to [spend-ledger.md](spend-ledger.md).
   107	
   108	**Provenance and attempts.** For every attempt, the complete effective prompt
   109	(`round-<N>-prompt.txt`), the full CLI log including the session header
   110	(model, session id, sandbox, reasoning effort) and token count
   111	(`round-<N>-codex.log`), and the final message are committed. An attempt that
   112	fails to return a verdict line (crash, timeout, quota) is retained as
   113	`round-<N>-attempt-<k>` and recorded in the ledger; at most one retry per round
   114	is allowed, and a second failure ends the round as having blocking objections
   115	unresolved. An attempt that returns a verdict cannot be discarded or rerun to
   116	obtain a different verdict. The reviewer reads the artifacts from the working
   117	tree at the recorded artifact SHA, and the tree must be clean (no uncommitted
   118	changes) when the call starts. The remaining trust is in the capture operator
   119	(the proposer's agent session): a fabricated log would be detectable only by
   120	comparing session ids and timestamps with the maintainer's local Codex
   121	session store (rounds 2 onward) and OpenAI usage records. This residual trust
   122	is disclosed, not eliminated.
   123	
   124	Round files follow [review-protocol.md](review-protocol.md):
   125	`reviews/R1/round-<N>-codex.md` (raw Codex output plus the disposition table,
   126	with the fixing commit for each accepted objection). Supporting files
   127	(prompts, logs, bindings, snapshots, the competence probe, and the
   128	countersignature record) live under `r1/`, because the R0 boundary test admits
   129	only `round-<N>-<reviewer>.md` files under `reviews/`.
   130	
   131	**Protocol conformance and variances.** The round limit (5, counting every
   132	reviewer), the rule that the maintainer cannot override an unresolved blocking
   133	objection, and the Copilot accounting (a Copilot review counts in the round of
   134	the commit it reviewed; reviewing any other artifact version consumes a round)
   135	are taken from the protocol unchanged. Two variances are deliberate and
   136	stricter: (1) from round 2, sessions are persisted rather than `--ephemeral`,
   137	so that the maintainer can verify provenance (section 7); (2) the reviewer runs
   138	with `--ignore-user-config` and reasoning effort `high`. From round 4, the
   139	prompt includes the protocol's reviewer instruction verbatim. Rounds 1-3 used
   140	a prompt that covered the same instructions in different words; this is
   141	disclosed as a protocol deviation for those rounds.
   142	
   143	**Binding file.** Before each call from round 3 on, the proposer commits
   144	`r1/round-<N>-bindings.sha256` in `shasum -a 256` check format. It
   145	lists the normative set (section 7), the complete effective prompt, and every
   146	authoritative dependency the reviewer is told to read or that the CLI loads as
   147	instructions:
   148	
   149	- `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` (repository instructions);
   150	- `r0-authorization.md`, `amendment-001-public-proxy.md`, and
   151	  `review-protocol.md` (R0 records);
   152	- `r1/snapshots/gate-state-at-review.json`, an immutable copy of
   153	  `gate-state.json` as reviewed (see "Live gate state" below);
   154	- `docs/plans/roadmap-v0.20-reference-draft-v3.md`, `docs/plans/roadmap-v0.20.md`;
   155	- `docs/plans/safe-delegation-m0/{authorization,decision,mechanism-and-resources,predecessor-status,supply-status}.md`;
   156	- `r1/snapshots/issue-{1278,1370,1372}.txt` (dated snapshots of the
   157	  GitHub issue text the rules cite; the reviewer has no network access, so these
   158	  snapshots are the issue text it reviewed).
   159	
   160	The R0 records are bound like every other dependency. If R0 (PR #1467) is
   161	merged with content that differs from the bound hashes, the binding check in
   162	section 7 fails, and R1 needs another review round or is not `MET`.
   163	
   164	**Live gate state.** `gate-state.json` is a live state file that later gates
   165	must update, so it is not hash-bound. Instead, at merge and at every later
   166	revalidation, the live file is compared with the bound snapshot
   167	`r1/snapshots/gate-state-at-review.json`. The only permitted differences are
   168	(a) the `value`, `effective`, and `record` fields of gate entries, and R5's
   169	classification, label, and action fields, changed by a merged record under
   170	the R0 transition rules; and (b) entries appended to `history`. Any other
   171	difference (authority, amendments, graph, prerequisites, limits, boundary
   172	flags, process states, classifications, or definitions) is a change to
   173	reviewed authority: R1 needs another review round or is not `MET`. The R1
   174	entry's `null -> MET` transition is an authorized transition of type (a) and is
   175	allowed only when section 7 items 1-5 hold.
   176	
   177	### 5.2 Scope of review
   178	
   179	The reviewer looks for rules that bias toward Calor, undefined or post-hoc
   180	adjustable thresholds, missing error control, missingness laundering
   181	(treating missing or invalid as success or as zero demand), ambiguous
   182	classifications, untestable criteria, and divergence from the original v0.20
   183	claim. The reviewer must not query public repository task data
   184	(rules section 9).
   185	
   186	### 5.3 Competence probe
   187	
   188	Before round 1, Codex reviews a short planted-defect rule set
   189	([r1/competence-probe.md](r1/competence-probe.md)) containing
   190	known defects. The result is recorded in that file. A reviewer that misses
   191	most planted defects is still used (no alternative exists), but the
   192	limitation is recorded and weakens the countersignature's evidentiary value.
   193	
   194	### 5.4 Budget
   195	
   196	At most 5 rounds per artifact, within the overall approximately USD 200 API
   197	cap and the 2026-10-29 window set by R0. Exceeding either ends R1 as
   198	`EXPIRED`.
   199	
   200	## 6. Recusal and rejected objections
   201	
   202	- Claude **cannot be final arbiter** of its own rejection of a **blocking**
   203	  objection. A rejected blocking objection is re-presented to Codex in the
   204	  next round with the rejection reason. If Codex withdraws or downgrades it,
   205	  the rejection stands.
   206	- If Codex maintains a blocking objection after rejection, or round 5 ends
   207	  with it unresolved, it goes to the maintainer, whose explicit decision is
   208	  recorded in `r1/countersignature.md`:
