   129	inquiry does not need individual identity, so:
   130	
   131	- Committed artifacts must not contain contributor usernames, display names,
   132	  or email addresses. Emails are never extracted.
   133	- Committed artifacts must not contain per-item identifiers that associate an
   134	  individual public object (a specific PR, issue, or commit) with an
   135	  eligibility decision, an exclusion, or a contributor cluster. A PR number or
   136	  commit SHA resolves to its author by direct lookup, so publishing it next to
   137	  a decision would identify the individual.
   138	- Provenance is carried instead by: the repository name and the pinned
   139	  repository revision (one snapshot SHA per repository, identifying a
   140	  repository state, not a contributor decision); the extraction window; the
   141	  pinned extractor code and eligibility rules; and a SHA-256 digest of the
   142	  full per-item decision list. The pinned extractor writes only aggregates
   143	  and the digest; the per-item list exists in memory or in local caches,
   144	  is never committed, and is deleted with local caches (7.3). Reproduction
   145	  re-runs the extractor and compares aggregates and digest.
   146	- **Scope of the anonymity rule.** The rule governs what this inquiry
   147	  publishes. It does not, and cannot, prevent a third party from re-deriving
   148	  per-item eligibility by running modified code over the same public
   149	  repositories, because every input is already public and attributable. The
   150	  inquiry does not claim anonymity against that reconstruction. The residual
   151	  is accepted because eligibility labels describe the technical shape of
   152	  public changes, not the people who made them, and the inquiry adds no
   153	  personal information to the public inputs.
   154	- Where an analysis needs author or reviewer clustering, it uses per-run
   155	  ordinal pseudonyms (`author-001`, ...) assigned in memory. The mapping from
   156	  pseudonym to username is never written to the repository or to persistent
   157	  storage, and is discarded when the run ends.
   158	- Published aggregates report no statistic computed over fewer than **5**
   159	  distinct contributors; such cells are merged or suppressed and the
   160	  suppression is stated.
   161	
   162	### 7.2 Retention versus deletion by category
   163	
   164	| Category | Expected content | Retained | Deletion trigger |
   165	|---|---|---|---|
   166	| Contact data | None (no contacts). Inbound exclusion requests only. | Request date and repository only; no handle, name, or message text | Not applicable (nothing personal retained) |
   167	| Restricted metadata | None permitted | Nothing | Immediate on discovery (7.3) |
   168	| Local caches (clones, API responses, scratch extracts, per-item decision lists) | Public data | Only while the gate that created them is active | Gate closure or any trigger in 7.3 |
   169	| Committed provenance records | Repository names, pinned revisions, extractor and rule versions, per-item list digests | As part of the public record | Repository exclusion request, license change that forbids the use, or discovery of personal/non-public data in the record |
   170	| Committed derived aggregates | Counts and estimates | Permanently, as the public record | Only if computed from data later found to be non-public or withdrawn; then marked invalid and recomputed or removed |
   171	| Logs | Review transcripts, spend ledger, disposition log | Permanently, as governance records, **except** that personal, non-public, or confidential content found in a log is removed (7.4); the log keeps a sanitized entry | Discovery of such content |
   172	| Consent records | None (no participants). Repository licenses are recorded in their place. | License identifier and revision per source | Not applicable |
   173	| Pseudonym maps | In memory only | Never persisted | End of each run |
   174	
   175	### 7.3 Triggers and deadlines
   176	
   177	All deadlines below are for **completed and verified** removal (7.6), not for
   178	opening a pull request.
   179	
   180	| Trigger | Action | Deadline |
   181	|---|---|---|
   182	| A gate closes with any value | Delete that gate's local caches | Within 7 days of closure |
   183	| Inquiry deadline passes or R0 becomes `EXPIRED` | Stop substantive work; Section 10 propagation; delete local caches | Stop immediately; deletion completed within 7 days |
   184	| R0 becomes `REVOKED` | Same as expiry | Same as expiry |
   185	| Milestone close (#1370 closed) | Delete any remaining local caches; committed public record stays | Within 7 days |
   186	| Repository owner or contributor asks for exclusion | Exclude the repository from all further analysis; remove its provenance rows; Section 10 invalidation of affected outputs | Stop use immediately; removal merged within 7 days; disposition published on #1370 within 7 days |
   187	| A source's license or terms stop permitting the use | Same as exclusion | Same as exclusion |
   188	| Non-public, restricted, personal, or confidential data is found in any cache, record, or log | Stop the run; delete local copies; remove from the repository, including history (7.4) | Stop immediately; local deletion and working-tree removal merged within 24 hours; history handling per 7.4 |
   189	| A gate is refused (`NOT_AUTHORIZED`) or authority is withdrawn | Inventory every access actually made under that gate (date, source, material) in the disposition log; delete its caches | Inventory within 3 days; deletion within 7 days |
   190	
   191	### 7.4 Retention exceptions and isolation
   192	
   193	- **Sensitive material overrides audit retention.** If personal, non-public,
   194	  or confidential material reached this public repository's history, the
   195	  maintainer rewrites the affected history (or, where a rewrite is
   196	  impossible, requests removal from the hosting service, including cached
   197	  views and forks it controls) and completes it within 14 days of discovery.
   198	  The disposition log records a sanitized entry: date, path, commit SHA of the
   199	  removal, and the category of material, never the material itself.
   200	- **Public-data history.** Public, non-sensitive material removed for any
   201	  other reason (for example an exclusion request) remains in git history. It
   202	  is isolated by a disposition-log entry naming the commit SHA, path, and
   203	  reason, and by the rule that no later analysis may read it as input. It is
   204	  disposed of only if the requester or a law requires; then the sensitive
   205	  rule above applies.
   206	- **Governance records.** Gate records, review transcripts, the spend ledger,
   207	  and the disposition log are retained as the audit trail. Invalidated or
   208	  expired evidence inside them may be retained only for audit and may not be
   209	  reused (#1370 lifecycle amendment).
   210	- No legal hold or other policy retention obligation is known on 2026-10-01.
   211	  If one arises, it is recorded in the disposition log with the same
   212	  isolation rule.
   213	
   214	### 7.5 Notification
   215	
   216	| Event | Who is notified | By whom | Deadline |
   217	|---|---|---|---|
   218	| Any change to access scope, confidentiality, or a source's permission (exclusion, license change, non-public discovery) | The maintainer | The agent or person who detects it, by stopping and recording the event in the active PR or gate record | Immediately, in the same session |
   219	| The same events | The public record: #1370 and the affected gate issue | The maintainer | Within 3 calendar days |
   220	| R0 `EXPIRED` or `REVOKED`; any gate `INVALIDATED` | The public record: #1370 and every affected gate issue | The maintainer | Within 3 calendar days |
   221	| Inbound exclusion request | The public record: #1370 (no reply to the requester under this authority) | The maintainer | Within 7 days |
   222	| Non-public third-party data accessed and a notice is legally required | Handled outside this inquiry (Section 3); the inquiry stops | The maintainer | As the law requires |
   223	
   224	### 7.6 Evidence of disposition
   225	
   226	Every deletion, revocation, isolation, or notification is evidenced by an
   227	appended entry in Section 13 giving: date, trigger, material, action taken,
   228	the command or PR/commit SHA that performed it, and a verification (for local
   229	deletion, the command and its output showing the path no longer exists; for
   230	history removal, the removal commit or the hosting service's confirmation;
   231	for notices, a link to the public comment or a dated note that a private
   232	notice was sent). Entries are sanitized: they never reproduce the removed
   233	material.
   234	
   235	## 8. Confidentiality, compensation, and conflicts
   236	
   237	**Confidentiality.** Nothing in the inquiry is confidential. All records are
   238	public. If confidential material is encountered, it is treated as a
   239	non-public-data discovery under 7.3.
   240	
   241	**Compensation.** None. No person is paid or offered anything. AI providers are
   242	paid only through existing subscriptions or within the Section 5 cash cap.
   243	
   244	**Conflicts.**
   245	
   246	| Party | Interest | Mitigation |
   247	|---|---|---|
   248	| @juanmicrosoft | Authors Calor and has an interest in a favorable outcome. Also holds decision, budget, and R5 classification authority, so these roles are not separated. | Cross-family adversarial review of every gate artifact; unresolved blocking objections prevent `MET` (protocol); mandatory "AI-adjudicated, public-proxy domain" label; no claim of independence |
   249	| Claude Code (proposer) | Its model family produced much of Calor's code and documentation, so it shares authorship bias with the subject under study. | It may not review its own artifacts as the required reviewer; a different model family must |
   250	| OpenAI (Codex) and GitHub (Copilot) | No stake in Calor's outcome. Their models share training-data overlap with the proposer and may share blind spots. The model behind Copilot review is not pinned and may not be a different family. | Codex is the required cross-family reviewer; Copilot is supplementary only; the shared-blind-spot risk is disclosed in every R5 output |
   251	
   252	## 9. Non-authorization boundary
   253	
   254	This authority does **not** permit, and no 0.23 gate may record as permitted,
   255	started, or performed:
   256	
   257	- participant enrollment or recruitment;
   258	- task execution (running study tasks with any agent or person);
   259	- paid experiment collection;
   260	- acceptance-service implementation, starter sets, workflows, or a held-out
   261	  oracle;
   262	- reuse of any 0.23 approval, funds, tasks, or evidence by #1254 or #1259;
   263	- reopening, satisfying, or changing the state of #1284-#1309;
   264	- any human contact beyond Section 3;
   265	- any non-public data access beyond Section 6.
   266	
   267	Even a later `FEASIBLE AS PROPOSED` does not activate implementation, spending,
   268	or recruitment.
   269	
   270	**Machine check.** [gate-state.json](gate-state.json) is the **only
   271	authoritative record** of gate values, boundary flags, and the R5
   272	classification. A Markdown statement that contradicts it has no effect.
   273	`tests/Calor.Compiler.Tests/Plans/SafeDelegationV023BoundaryTests.cs` fails
   274	if:
   275	
   276	- any boundary flag is missing or not `false`, or an unknown flag is not
   277	  `false`;
   278	- the gate set differs from the fixed amended graph (R0, R1, R2A′, R3, R2B′,
   279	  R4, R5), or any gate's prerequisite list differs from that graph (the
   280	  test holds its own copy, so editing the JSON cannot loosen it);
   281	- any gate value is outside the #1370 process-state vocabulary;
   282	- any gate is `MET` while a prerequisite is not `MET`;
   283	- the original-gate history values (R1, R2A, R3, R2B, R4 as filed) are
   284	  removed or changed;
   285	- R5 holds a formal classification without the label
   286	  "AI-adjudicated, public-proxy domain", or R5 is `MET` without both a
   287	  formal classification and a separate maintainer action;
   288	- the cash cap exceeds USD 200, the round cap exceeds 5, or an hours cap
   289	  exceeds 20 (maintainer) or 60 (agent sessions);
   290	- a review file is misnamed or exceeds round 5.
   291	
   292	It also scans Markdown under `v0.23/` (outside correctly named review
   293	transcripts, which quote reviewer output verbatim) for two declaration forms,
   294	joining wrapped lines within a paragraph:
   295	
   296	- a classification declaration (`classification:` or a table cell) naming one
   297	  of the four formal classifications without the required label;
   298	- a prohibited activity (participant enrollment or recruitment, task
   299	  execution, paid collection, acceptance-service implementation) followed by
   300	  a colon or table cell and a state word such as `authorized` or `started`,
   301	  or followed by `is`/`was`/`has been` and such a word, unless a negation
   302	  (`no`, `not`, `never`, `without`, `prohibit`) precedes it in the sentence.
   303	
   304	**Limits of the machine check.** Prose scanning is heuristic. A sufficiently
   305	rephrased sentence can evade it, and it cannot judge the truth of a record.
   306	Its purpose is to catch accidental declarations; the authoritative state is
   307	the validated JSON, and the adversarial review protocol remains the control
   308	for meaning.
