     1	# Roadmap - v0.20 "Earn the Language"
     2	
     3	**Created:** 2026-09-08
     4	
     5	**Status:** Draft v3, focused revision after two critique rounds. Decision-first proposal;
     6	no prototype, experiment, recruitment commitment, or spending authorized.
     7	
     8	**Release sequence:** v0.18 finishes its current work; v0.19 addresses the
     9	[language audit epic #1182](https://github.com/juanmicrosoft/calor/issues/1182)
    10	and [website audit epic #1202](https://github.com/juanmicrosoft/calor/issues/1202).
    11	This roadmap evaluates the investment proposed for v0.20; a decision-only
    12	stop does not trigger a package release or consume that version number.
    13	
    14	**Decision owner:** repository maintainer. An independent reviewer must sign
    15	off on the comparison and final interpretation before any advantage claim.
    16	
    17	## 1. The decision, in plain language
    18	
    19	> Can a team let an agent change important business rules in Calor at half
    20	> the total cost per correctly accepted change, without materially worse
    21	> safety or completion, compared with well-equipped C#?
    22	
    23	Here "half" means an upper confidence bound at or below 0.50, not just a
    24	favorable point estimate. The true improvement needed for an affordable
    25	study to pass depends on variance; no evidence currently establishes it.
    26	
    27	The comparison must include C# with protected requirements and enforcement,
    28	not just ordinary C#. Otherwise we could prove that checking rules helps
    29	without proving that a new language helps.
    30	
    31	v0.20 ends with one of three investment decisions: **continue Calor in this
    32	narrow domain, move the useful workflow into C# tooling, or stop expanding
    33	this bet**. Inconclusive evidence is a reason to stop the investment under
    34	this budget, not evidence that the scientific claim is false.
    35	
    36	"Once and for all" means making this investment decision against a frozen,
    37	affordable experiment. It cannot mean proving that one language beats every
    38	other language on every application or future model.
    39	
    40	### This is not a new thesis
    41	
    42	[Agent-native strategy](agent-native-strategy.md), section 0, already proposes
    43	safer agent changes and reduced review burden. Its section 2 already says
    44	the product is the workflow. Repeating that thesis is not progress.
    45	
    46	This roadmap adds a strong C# workflow control, protected acceptance rules,
    47	end-to-end adoption economics, and a finite decision deadline. It does not
    48	relabel old mechanism results as user benefit.
    49	
    50	The [v0.18 roadmap](roadmap-v0.18.md) measures a narrower effect-row claim.
    51	Record its actual outcome as input; do not presume a win or use it as a
    52	substitute for this experiment.
    53	
    54	### First decision: can we afford to answer this?
    55	
    56	**M0 is a desk feasibility screen before implementation or external
    57	commitments.** The initial arithmetic in section 6.3 does not justify
    58	scheduling the full comparison under the proposed resource envelope.
    59	M0 first sizes the original gates, then maps conditional alternatives
    60	without approving them. Its four outputs are defined in section 6.3.
    61	No output itself authorizes implementation or changes the safety claim.
    62	
    63	An independently approved exploratory study is another option, not a
    64	fallback pass. It may measure friction or disprove a mechanism on an
    65	in-house example, but cannot declare safe-delegation superiority, justify
    66	production adoption, or automatically fund a larger acceptance service.
    67	Section 7 specifies the separate authorization that option needs.
    68	
    69	## 2. Questions that must receive answers
    70	
    71	| Question | Evidence required | Consequence |
    72	|---|---|---|
    73	| What painful work are we improving? | A non-maintainer adopter's recurring rule changes, current review process, and expected change volume | No adopter means demand unproven; stop before building the workflow |
    74	| Does enforcement improve agent work? | Protected C# versus ordinary C#, with the same requirements | Separates workflow value from language value |
    75	| Does Calor add enough beyond that workflow? | Calor versus both C# arms on total cost, safety, and completion | Only this comparison can earn continued language investment |
    76	| Can we trust the acceptance boundary? | Production-path counterexamples, bypass checks, and independent output evaluation | A false guarantee blocks collection or invalidates the affected epoch |
    77	| Are we moving work onto humans? | Specification, integration, review, repair, and training time, including failed tasks | All enter cost; no free annotation or review labor |
    78	| Is the result useful outside a fixture? | Real task provenance, unseen tasks, and a non-maintainer handoff | Synthetic mechanism wins alone cannot pass |
    79	| What happens if the result is weak? | Precommitted outcome table and deadline | No automatic next release of feature expansion |
    80	
    81	## 3. Scope: one small part of an existing C# application
    82	
    83	**Proposed first domain: refund eligibility and amount decisions.** Confirm
    84	an adopter with this need in M1. If none exists, one alternative among
    85	entitlement, pricing, or routing decisions may be selected on documented
    86	user need **before any comparative pilot**. No cycling through domains
    87	until Calor wins.
    88	
    89	The host remains C#. Calor handles a bounded decision module: explicit
    90	inputs, a decision output, and no execution of payments or database writes.
    91	Use integer minor units for money, with explicit ranges and overflow rules.
    92	Time, tenant identity, and policy data arrive as explicit validated inputs.
    93	
    94	Example protected requirements:
    95	
    96	- A proposed refund never exceeds the remaining refundable amount.
    97	- A request cannot produce a decision for a different tenant.
    98	- Evaluating the policy cannot perform external I/O or mutate host state.
    99	
   100	The tenant rule covers the modeled input/output boundary, not all database
   101	access in the application. The host must authenticate the tenant and supply
   102	the correct records. Payment execution, transaction isolation, concurrent
   103	refunds, and correctness of external services are outside the initial proof
   104	boundary. Include these assumptions in every evidence report.
   105	
   106	Start with immutable values, bounded integer calculations, branches, and
   107	statically resolved calls whose summaries can be checked. M1 publishes the
   108	exact supported construct/property matrix against the v0.19 compiler.
   109	Unsupported constructs are refused by the protected workflow, not assumed
   110	safe. Do not require a heap model or general-purpose C# parity to enter.
   111	
   112	**Not in scope:** a new syntax redesign, broad migration, a new IDE,
   113	whole-application proofs, distributed-system correctness, or making the
   114	benchmark's composite advantage score larger.
   115	
   116	### Research participation is not production adoption
   117	
   118	Use isolated copies, historical tasks, and non-production integration for
   119	this experiment. A signed research agreement does not authorize a team to
   120	put Calor in a live refund path.
   121	
   122	[Call W's disposition](call-w-adjudication.md) records DEMAND UNPROVEN and
   123	keeps Call 3 closed. The [loop plan](loop-plan-v0.9.md), sections 6.2-6.3,
   124	records the external-adoption decision and its continuing obligations.
   125	M1 needs an explicit maintainer governance disposition before recruiting a
   126	committed adopter: how this research relates to Call 3, which prior
   127	conditions apply, and what remains closed. Do not silently supersede that
   128	decision by naming recruitment a milestone.
   129	
   130	The research agreement must state the possible negative outcomes, duration
   131	of support, maintenance/compatibility limits, data permissions, withdrawal
   132	procedure, and funded exit assistance. Production adoption, if later sought,
   133	requires separate Call 3 approval. No experiment participant is required
   134	to depend on the continued development of Calor.
   135	
   136	The exit deliverable is a runnable C# handoff with behavioral comparison
   137	against the independently approved requirements. Document lost static
   138	guarantees, remaining runtime checks, and dependencies. Record its actual
   139	test status in M1 and demonstrate it before the adopter handoff; do not
   140	promise "lossless" export merely because emission succeeds.
   141	
   142	## 4. Product prototype: protected change, not editable promises
   143	
   144	### 4.1 Separate authority from implementation
   145	
   146	The agent may edit the implementation and its own supplementary tests.
   147	It may propose requirement changes, but cannot approve them.
   148	
   149	A separate trusted acceptance service owns the requirement bundle,
   150	dependency/effect summaries, compiler and solver pins, build configuration,
   151	allowed file surface, and final acceptance decision. Agent workspace hooks
   152	alone are not the protection: the service rebuilds from approved inputs in
   153	an isolated environment and checks the actual submitted artifact.
   154	
   155	Protect generated C#, project/build files, referenced packages, analyzers,
   156	proof caches, runner configuration, requirement files, and acceptance
   157	scripts against agent-controlled substitution. Trusted checks come from
   158	the protected baseline, not executable scripts in the proposed patch.
   159	No privileged service credentials or hidden evaluation assets enter the
   160	agent's environment.
   161	
   162	The service binds evidence to source, requirements, dependencies, toolchain,
   163	and configuration by content hash. A change to any relevant input makes
   164	previous evidence stale. Record the exact artifact accepted for deployment;
   165	do not approve one tree and deploy another.
   166	
   167	### 4.2 Small requirements, independently approved
   168	
   169	Requirements express stable business limits, not a second implementation.
   170	The adopter approves them before agents implement the changes. All arms
   171	receive the same natural-language specification and semantic constraints.
   172	Translation into arm-specific forms is reviewed and charged to that arm.
   173	
   174	Check that preconditions are satisfiable and that valid business inputs
   175	remain admitted. Always returning "deny", narrowing the input domain, or
   176	throwing on every request is not success. Independent behavioral checks
   177	include positive business cases and behavior not fully described by the
   178	protected contracts.
   179	
   180	The intended task can legitimately change a business requirement. In that
   181	case the task starts with the same independently approved replacement
   182	bundle for every arm. Unauthorized weakening during implementation fails.
   183	
   184	### 4.3 Honest outcomes and useful repair feedback
   185	
   186	Present **established**, **violated**, and **not established**, mapping the
   187	existing detailed proof statuses without throwing their distinctions away.
   188	Timeout, unsupported analysis, missing dependencies, and stale evidence
   189	remain distinct machine-readable reasons under "not established".
   190	
   191	An established result names the exact properties, modeled domain,
   192	assumptions, and trusted dependencies. It is never "the application is safe".
   193	A violation supplies a concrete counterexample when available. If a fact
   194	cannot be established, the strict workflow does not automatically accept.
   195	Human exception handling is recorded as escalation, with its full cost;
   196	it cannot count as machine-established evidence.
   197	
   198	Compose dependency summaries only where the compiler checks the relevant
   199	call, argument, state, and effect relationships. Unanalyzed code is not a
   200	free pure summary. Cross-module edits invalidate dependent evidence.
   201	
   202	### 4.4 Prototype acceptance criteria
   203	
   204	The prototype must reject unauthorized rule/configuration changes, raw-C#
   205	escape attempts, changed dependencies, and stale proof reuse. Deliberately
   206	bad implementations must fail; legitimate changes must pass. Exercise the
   207	same production compilation and acceptance paths used in the experiment.
   208	
   209	Every #1182 finding whose construct or property appears in M1's supported
   210	matrix must have a discriminating regression that fails on its pre-fix
   211	baseline and passes on the pinned compiler. Include false postcondition
   212	proofs, simplification, assignment effects, expression grouping, match-arm
   213	execution, and numeric semantics (#1183-#1185 and #1187-#1189). Include
   214	inherited contracts (#1186) if interfaces/inheritance are admitted; otherwise
   215	enforce their exclusion. Apply the same rule to all other epic findings.
   216	Issue closure is not a substitute for these artifacts.
   217	Keep runtime guards in this prototype; retaining guards does not repair an
   218	unsound proof, so false-established results still block the experiment.
   219	
   220	Budget a separate search for false-established results over the complete
   221	supported matrix, including feature interactions, mutation, and dependency
   222	changes. The oracle is a violated **claimed property** on a modeled input
   223	while that property remains established. Changed behavior alone is not a
   224	false proof: different behaviors may satisfy the same contract. Retain
   225	behavior-preserving and contract-preserving mutations as negative controls.
   226	Zero findings establish only that these probes found none, not soundness
   227	of the entire compiler.
   228	
   229	## 5. Experiment: three credible workflows
   230	
   231	| Arm | Language and tooling | Protection |
   232	|---|---|---|
   233	| A: ordinary C# | Current C#, Roslyn/NRT/analyzers, normal tests, and agent-generated tests or other practical evidence | Common protected requirements and runner; normal review, no new domain-specific proof gate |
   234	| B: protected C# | A plus a credible implementation of the protected-rule workflow using analyzers, verification tools, restricted patterns, and/or runtime enforcement | Same independently approved rules and authority separation as Calor |
   235	| C: protected Calor | Calor in the same C# host, using the supported contracts/effects and protected acceptance prototype | Same independently approved rules and authority separation as B |
   236	
   237	All arms may use practical existing tools, including formal tools in C#.
   238	Do not require B to analyze unrestricted C#: it may use the same restrictions
   239	as C. Do not ban a good C# solution because it makes Calor less distinctive.
   240	
   241	M0 sketches B using property-based testing, runtime guards, the shared
   242	acceptance service, and Roslyn/static restrictions on I/O, host mutation,
   243	and nondeterminism. A banned-API list alone is not an effect system:
   244	the design must address wrappers, transitive calls, and unanalyzed code.
   245	M1 requires a C#-experienced non-maintainer to approve B's design and tool
   246	choices **before C's integration starts**, not merely endorse the result.
   247	Publish the review's concrete acceptance criteria and disclose conflicts.
   248	
   249	Budget B and C integration separately from the shared service, with equal
   250	integration allowances, actual effort logs, and Calor's sunk investment
   251	reported separately. Equal new effort does not erase existing maturity
   252	differences: this measures deployable workflows, not equal research effort.
   253	If credible B cannot be delivered within its allowance, the comparison is
   254	**not ready**; that is not evidence Calor won. M0 states the proposed true
   255	cost advantage and why it is plausible; do not invent that expectation from
   256	a desired sample size or imply that previous effect-row results support it.
   257	
   258	Expose equivalent requirement content and tool guidance. Equal information
   259	does not require identical syntax or identical diagnostics. Those differences
   260	are part of the workflows being measured. Do not claim pure syntax causation.
   261	
   262	### 5.1 Tasks and independent correctness
   263	
   264	Use real changes or independently authored requests grounded in the
   265	adopter's recorded work. Freeze the sampling frame and inclusion criteria
   266	before selecting confirmatory tasks. Include ordinary changes, boundary
   267	cases, cross-function changes, valid rule updates, and tasks outside the
   268	protected subset at registered proportions representative of that work.
   269	Report eligible versus excluded work across the entire sampling frame.
   270	
   271	Freeze the primary task mix in M1: **at least 75% of primary task weight
   272	on distinct replayed historical requests; at most 25% on authored variants**.
   273	Count each source request as one independent task cluster; related variants
   274	and repeated agent runs do not increase the independent-task count. Keep
   275	source clusters disjoint between pilot and final pools. Report historical
   276	and authored outcomes separately as well as under the frozen task weights.
   277	
   278	Use the adopter's preceding 12-month history as the initial sampling frame.
   279	M0 reports available inventory or labels scenario counts as unverified;
   280	M1 confirms actual eligible requests before authorizing implementation.
   281	Record source IDs/hashes, exclusions, usable historical count, independently
   282	authored clusters, source linkage, and pilot/final reservations. Apply the
   283	75% historical-weight floor to each pool. Missing supply cannot be filled
   284	with correlated variants. The 50-future-request adoption minimum is neither
   285	a historical measurement nor a ceiling; section 6.3 accounts for actual supply.
   286	Historical implementations and incident reports inform the independent
   287	oracle but are not automatically correct reference answers.
   288	
   289	Keep mutation-injected failures and deliberate bypass probes as a separate
   290	mechanism suite. Do not pool them with natural tasks to inflate real-world
   291	defect rates. Do not make tasks harder merely because strong C# succeeds.
   292	Screen whether the primary economic and joint safety/completion gates can
   293	distinguish the workflows; do not impose a C# failure-rate quota. Equal
   294	100% completion does not prevent a meaningful cost difference.
   295	
   296	Author idiomatic starters from the shared behavioral specification.
   297	Independently compare input/output behavior before admitting a task.
   298	Mechanical C# -> Calor conversion is optional, not the correctness oracle;
   299	exclude conversion defects from measurement only before freezing the suite.
   300	
   301	A non-implementing evaluator owns one arm-shared black-box held-out suite
   302	and severity rubric, including hidden regression and property cases.
   303	Reference solutions must satisfy it. The evaluator and acceptance service
   304	are separate: accepted artifacts can fail held-out evaluation.
   305	No implementation agent or acceptance reviewer receives held-out feedback
   306	until the entire confirmatory epoch is sealed.
   307	Before freeze, archive five consecutive successful runs of each reference
   308	solution against its held-out suite, including the pins and determinism
   309	evidence. This checks the instrument, not the completeness of its oracle.
   310	
   311	### 5.2 Assignment, reviewers, and execution
   312	
   313	Use one pinned primary model/agent configuration in all arms. Freeze model
   314	ID, settings, tool versions, hardware, dependency versions, prices, and
   315	time/token/iteration budgets. Randomize arm execution order and pair runs
   316	by task. A second model is optional descriptive replication only; it cannot
   317	rescue a primary failure. A model change mid-epoch invalidates comparison.
   318	Explicitly pin solver version, per-query and total solver time limits,
   319	CPU/memory limits, and cache policy. Solver time is ordinary workflow cost,
   320	including in B if B uses a solver; a timeout is not a free infrastructure retry.
   321	
   322	Model familiarity with C# is a registered limitation of generalization and
   323	a real part of today's adoption cost, not a reason to excuse a Calor loss.
   324	Report syntax/compile-error repair separately from logic/contract repair
   325	using a frozen classification, retaining mixed/unknown categories. Do not
   326	assert familiarity is the dominant cause without evidence.
   327	
   328	Each arm/task/repetition starts with a fresh agent conversation and isolated
   329	working tree. No shared agent memory, earlier solutions, repair transcripts,
   330	or cross-arm artifacts are available. Freeze permitted persistent resources
   331	(for example package caches and approved documentation) and an identical
   332	cold/warm-cache policy; charge setup and cache preparation consistently.
   333	Use fresh protected proof caches per slot except for reuse within that slot.
   334	
   335	Recruit at least three qualified non-maintainer reviewers for timed review.
   336	Train them on each workflow; charge training time. Counterbalance assignment
   337	and ensure a reviewer does not see the same underlying task in multiple arms.
   338	Freeze a feasible assignment schedule, including repeated-run handling and
   339	pilot/final separation, within the total reviewer-hours ceiling.
   340	The M1 role/assignment table must cover specification approval, B-design
   341	review, three timed reviewers, independent oracle ownership, and final
   342	interpretation. People may cover compatible roles, but an implementer cannot
   343	own the hidden oracle or independently judge their own artifact. Do not
   344	assume five to seven distinct people are already available.
   345	Language cannot be blinded, so disclose that limitation. Hide arm labels
   346	from the independent behavioral/severity adjudication wherever possible.
   347	
   348	Reviewers use the evidence available in that arm and may accept, reject, or
   349	request repair under the same time cap. Log active review, diagnosis, repair,
   350	and escalation time. No automatic Calor acceptance while forcing a full
   351	line-by-line review in C#: each workflow gets its natural evidence-based
   352	review. A and B may also earn quick acceptance.
   353	
   354	Evaluate the final immutable artifact after the ordinary review/repair
   355	workflow ends. Do not let hidden tests guide further repair. Preserve
   356	pre-review outcomes as secondary evidence to distinguish agent performance
   357	from human rescue.
   358	
   359	An **assigned slot** is one task, arm, and preregistered repetition. It has
   360	one fixed total budget, including ordinary tool calls, review, and repair.
   361	Agents may recover from compiler errors, solver failures, or unsupported
   362	constructs within that budget without changing the protected requirements.
   363	A terminal refusal/crash, exhausted budget, or unresolved escalation is a
   364	failed slot with its full cost. There is only one final accepted artifact
   365	per slot; intermediate candidates are not accepted for deployment.
   366	
   367	Only independently detected shared infrastructure failures may replace an
   368	execution, with at most two replacement executions per paired slot under
   369	frozen rules applied symmetrically to all arms. Retain every execution and
   370	charge its cost to the original slot. Replacements never add denominator
   371	entries, and a genuine accepted-artifact failure cannot be relabeled as an
   372	infrastructure error. An exhausted slot remains a failure. After freeze,
   373	no exclusions based on which arm lost.
