   505	### 6.3 Size first; calibrate only if a credible route exists
   506	
   507	#### M0 desk screen: conditional outputs, no implementation authority
   508	
   509	These are single-rate, zero-event illustrations, not Calor results or the
   510	paired risk-difference design. For n independent binary observations the
   511	upper bound is `1 - alpha^(1/n)`:
   512	
   513	| Independent observations n | Descriptive 95% bound | Branch-level 97.5% illustration |
   514	|---|---|---|
   515	| 20 | 13.91% | 16.84% |
   516	| 30 | 9.50% | 11.57% |
   517	| 50 | 5.82% | 7.11% |
   518	| 100 | 2.95% | 3.62% |
   519	| 299 | <= 1.00% | 1.23% |
   520	| 368 | 0.81% | <= 1.00% |
   521	
   522	The 1% crossovers are 299 and 368 observations respectively. For scale,
   523	an unpaired approximation with equal 3% event rates, zero true difference,
   524	1-point margin, alpha 0.025 and 80% power gives about 4,570 per arm:
   525	`n = (1.960 + 0.842)^2 * 2 * 0.03 * 0.97 / 0.01^2`.
   526	Pairing, accepted-only denominators, and clustering require the actual
   527	methods, not substitution of these figures as final sample sizes.
   528	
   529	Capacity and task supply are separate constraints. At 20 minutes per slot,
   530	40 available review hours buy 40 three-arm triplets with one repetition;
   531	all 80 hours buy 80, before pilot reservations and other duties. If actual
   532	eligible historical supply H is 50, the 75% historical-weight floor allows
   533	at most `floor(H / 0.75) = 66` equal-weight clusters across both pools,
   534	before further source-linkage restrictions. This is an upper bound, not a
   535	promise that independent authored clusters exist. Final capacity must also
   536	subtract pilot history and review use. **H has not been measured here**;
   537	the minimum 50 future requests does not imply H = 50.
